      {
        "review_id": 549655455,
        "review_link": "https://www.tripadvisor.com/ShowUserReviews-g293890-d310712-r549655455-Pashupatinath_Temple-Kathmandu_Kathmandu_Valley_Bagmati_Zone_Central_Region.html",
        "title": "Eye-opening Experience ",
        "text": "Upon my arrival at Kathmandu, I passed by the Pashupatinath on my way from the airport to the city centre.  The taxi driver commented that the smoke in the air was from the cremation ceremony.  Having read about the no holds barred open display of the cremation of bodies, I was not sure whether I was “up to it” to visit the temple.\n \nAfter spending 11 days in Kathmandu/ Pokhara/ Ghorepani-Poonhill, I finally decided to include visiting the Pashupatinath along with the Boudhanath and the Swayambhunath on the day before I fly back to Kuala Lumpur.  I hired a driver for the day because I did not want to go through the hassle of bargaining with multiple taxi drivers to get from one place to another.  Nevertheless, a one-way taxi ride from Thamel to Pashupatinath should cost about NPR400 or less (if you are good in haggling).\n \nAt the time of my visit, the entrance fees to this UNESCO World Heritage Site was NPR1,000.  Remember to keep your ticket because you are required to produce your ticket at the other entrance to the ritual cremation area.  At the ticket booth, you would encounter a number of guides touting their services.  While you are not obliged to engage their services, having a guide to show you around the complex could be helpful. \n \nEven though I was the only Asian visitor that morning, most of the Hindu worshippers greeted me with kind smiles.  The main grounds of the Pashupatinath temple are off-limits to non-Hindus.  There’s quite a bit to see around the vicinity outside the off-limits grounds.  After exploring that part of the complex, the next on the agenda would probably be witnessing a public cremation.\n \nHailing from a multi-racial country, I have a deep respect for the Hindu religion and culture.  My respect increased many folds after watching the process of the bodies being prepared for cremation and the cremation process itself.  This is very different from the wakes and the funerals which I have attended.  What I assumed was unnerving turned out to be a visible act of love by the family members to bid a final farewell to send off their loved ones.\n \nSmoke and open display of the heads and feet of the bodies aside, I am humbled to be able to witness this meaningful ritual.",
